# Wasla Core Rules

Wasla is a focused multi-tenant SaaS for restaurant online-order operations.

## Product scope

Current scope:
- One operational screen for platform orders.
- Live kitchen display.
- Automatic receipt creation/printing.
- Wasla Print Bridge.
- Tenant signup, payment, and provisioning flow.
- Per-tenant settings and platform connections.

Out of scope unless explicitly requested:
- Full POS, full restaurant ERP, inventory/stock management, accounting, payroll, broad CRM, or unrelated restaurant management modules.

Keep solutions practical, conservative, and MVP-focused.

## Project structure

- `Wasla.Web`: ASP.NET Core MVC/Razor public, signup, tenant, and admin web UI.
- `Wasla.Api`: JSON endpoints, integrations, and webhooks where applicable.
- `Wasla.Worker`: background provider synchronization.
- `Wasla.Cli`: provisioning, maintenance, administrative tooling, and EF startup project.
- `Wasla.Application`: use cases, commands, queries, interfaces, and results.
- `Wasla.Infrastructure`: EF Core, DbContexts, migrations, repositories, persistence, integrations, encryption, secrets, and email.
- `Wasla.Domain`: entities, enums, and core business concepts.
- `Wasla.Contracts`: API request/response contracts only.
- `Wasla.PrintBridge`: Print Bridge client for local printing.

## Architecture context

- Wasla uses a database-per-tenant model.
- `CentralDb` stores tenant metadata, memberships, signup/provisioning state, and central configuration.
- `TenantDb` stores operational restaurant data.
- EF Core contexts are `CentralDbContext` and `TenantDbContext`.
- Tenant resolution uses `TenantResolutionMiddleware`, claim `TenantId`, and HttpContext item `CurrentTenant`.
- Host roles (WAS-66, `docs/architecture/tenancy.md`): marketing `wasla.tr` (Development `localhost`, `wasla.local`, `www.wasla.local`), application entry `app.wasla.tr` (Development `app.wasla.local`), tenants `{slug}.app.wasla.tr` (Development `{slug}.app.wasla.local`, for example `sushim.app.wasla.local`).
- Classify hosts with `WaslaHostClassifier`; never match tenant hosts with `Contains`, loose `EndsWith` or unvalidated splitting.
- End-customer order fields such as `CustomerName`, `CustomerPhone`, and `CustomerAddress` refer to the restaurant's buyer and should not be renamed to Tenant.

## Git workflow

- `master` is the stable branch.
- `dev` is the long-running active development branch.
- Normal development and bug fixes must be performed on `dev`.
- Do not work directly on `master`.
- Do not create a new feature branch, task branch, or `cursor/*` branch unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Before changing files, always run `git branch --show-current` and `git status`.
- If the current branch is not `dev`, stop and report it unless the user explicitly requested another branch.
- Cursor Agent conversation/session names are not the source of truth for the active Git branch.
- The source of truth is `git branch --show-current`.
- A Cloud or Local Agent must not assume it is on `dev` merely because the prompt says so.
- Do not commit unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not push unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not merge `dev` into `master` unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not stash, discard, reset, clean, delete, or overwrite uncommitted work without explicit approval.
- Keep unrelated changes out of the current task.
- Use small, focused commits when the user asks for a commit.
- Do not include generated files, temporary scripts, local configuration, certificates, secrets, or scratch files in commits.
- Before committing, show the changed files and confirm task scope.
- Before merging to `master`, build and test the relevant flow.

## General rules

- Do not add unrelated features.
- Do not change database schema unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not add migrations unless explicitly requested.
- Do not reset migrations unless explicitly requested.
- Do not change provider behavior unless the task is explicitly about providers.
- Do not change order lifecycle behavior unless explicitly requested.
- Do not introduce React, Vue, or heavy frontend build tooling for MVP.
- Prefer ASP.NET Core MVC/Razor, Bootstrap 5, jQuery/vanilla JS, and existing project conventions.
- Preserve existing behavior unless the task explicitly asks for behavior change.
- If a task fails, report the exact project, file/class, config key, and exception/error.

## Build and test

- After code changes, run `dotnet build Wasla.sln`.
- In Linux/Cloud environments where Windows-targeted projects are part of the solution, use `dotnet build Wasla.sln -p:EnableWindowsTargeting=true`.
- Do not claim browser/end-to-end tests passed when only static code review or ad-hoc assertions were performed.
- Clearly distinguish build verification, automated tests, static code review, and real browser/manual testing.
- If live testing is blocked by LocalDB, SQL Server, certificates, or platform limitations, report the blocker honestly.
- Do not add temporary test scripts to production code.
- Remove scratch files before presenting a final diff.
- Do not silently fix unrelated issues discovered during testing.

## Naming and legacy compatibility

- Canonical names and the clean-break policy live in `docs/product/terminology.md`; follow it.
- New code, configuration, documentation and tests use `Wasla` product naming and `Tenant` for tenant concepts. Restaurant-facing UI says "Restaurant" in the localized form.
- `Customer` naming is valid only for the real person placing an order (for example `CustomerName`, `CustomerPhone`, `CustomerAddress`, customer notes).
- Do not reintroduce the former product name or tenant-meaning Customer names anywhere, including tests, in any form (literal, fragmented, encoded, character classes or reconstructed at runtime).
- Add no compatibility layer for retired names: no aliases, fallback keys, dual reads or writes, redirects for retired routes, old cookie names, old paths, classic Print Bridge fallback or compatibility commands.
- Clean-break tests assert the canonical surface positively; no negative tests that name retired identifiers and no test-file allowlists.
- Retired names that still exist are removed by their owning clean-break issue, not during unrelated fixes.
- The exact historical migration files listed in `docs/product/terminology.md` are the only exception. Do not edit them, do not add files to that list, and do not generate migrations that contain retired names.
- Internal settings should use current canonical property names.
- For Print Bridge, use `ServerUrl`, not new `BaseUrl` aliases.

## Print Bridge

- Product name: `Wasla Print Bridge`.
- ProgramData path: `C:\ProgramData\Wasla\PrintBridge`.
- Device authentication uses `X-PrintBridge-Token`.
- The desktop app must not require the user to know internal API paths.
- Manual setup requires Wasla Web Panel URL / `ServerUrl` and Device Token.
- `ServerUrl` is a primary setup value and must not be hidden as an advanced-only value.
- Empty `ServerUrl` and empty device token must be blocked locally before a request.
- Trim setup values before saving.
- Save must reconnect/ping using the newly saved values.
- Do not hardcode production URLs or secrets.
- Do not attempt token-based server discovery unless a central discovery service is explicitly designed.
- Manual setup must remain available even if automatic protocol setup is added.
- Do not expose full tokens in logs.
- Token should be masked in UI by default.
- Device name is managed from the Wasla Web panel/token identity; local machine name may be shown separately.
- Preserve current polling defaults unless explicitly changed: Idle 5 seconds, Busy 1 second, Error 15 seconds, Max jobs per poll 3.
- Print flow is Pending -> Printing -> Printed/Failed.
- Do not print directly from Web or Worker.
- Local Windows Print Bridge is responsible for physical printing.

## Signup, payment, and provisioning

- Business email may be optional.
- Empty/whitespace optional email is valid; non-empty email must be validated.
- Owner email remains required where the current model requires it.
- Payment success and provisioning are separate states.
- A known `PendingRegistration` with successful payment must not be shown as an unknown tenant.
- Status UX: unknown slug -> tenant-not-found; awaiting payment -> pending/payment CTA; payment succeeded -> panel being prepared; provisioned -> panel ready/login.
- Do not create a tenant DB from tenant resolution.
- Do not provision from middleware.
- Do not manually mark a registration as Provisioned.
- Provisioning must remain payment-gated.
- Post-provision email must not roll back successful provisioning if the current implementation treats email as best-effort.
- Do not expose password hashes, tokens, or secrets in status pages.
- Standalone signup/payment/status pages may use a minimal layout without the public header.
- Tenant address links and central homepage links must use URL builders rather than relative-host assumptions.
- On tenant-hosted pending pages, "Back to homepage" must point to the central/public Wasla host, not `/` on the tenant host.

## Naming and language

- Routes, folders, classes, methods, and code identifiers should remain English.
- Visible UI is localized and Turkish-friendly by default.
- Do not hardcode new user-facing strings.
